// Orisha in a unikernel — the source is ready, the LINK IS NOT. Read this
// before quoting the example as working.
//
// Nothing in this file is unikernel-specific, and that part is real. The
// handler and `serve` are the same two lines a hosted Orisha program writes;
// what changes is one build flag, which selects a different `~proc run|…`
// inside orisha/pump and nothing else. That is the pump seam doing its job.
//
//   koruc main.kz --build=unikraft        ✅ works — emits output_emitted.zig
//
//   zig build-lib wrapper.zig -target x86_64-freestanding \
//       -O ReleaseSmall -fno-stack-protector -femit-bin=libkoruapp.a
//                                          ❌ FAILS TODAY, two errors:
//
//     std/heap.zig:57: freestanding/other page_size_max must provided with
//                      std.options.page_size_max
//       reached through std.heap.PageAllocator — Orisha asks for a host page
//       allocator at module level, and a machine with no OS has no pages to ask
//       about.
//
//     output_emitted.zig:87: C import failed — 'time.h' file not found
//       `const time_c = @cImport(@cInclude("time.h"))` in orisha/lib/index.kz,
//       for the HTTP Date header. No libc on this target.
//
// Both are ORISHA'S, not the compiler's or Unikraft's, and both want the same
// treatment the readiness loop already got: a seam with a body per platform.
// Until they land, this directory builds a Linux binary and nothing boots.
//
// ⚠️ THEREFORE: Orisha CANNOT BE BENCHMARKED IN A UNIKERNEL YET. The image that
// does boot and serve is koru/examples/unikraft-net, which is not Orisha — it is
// a hand-written Koru program whose proc answers with a hardcoded 18-byte string
// and stops after three requests (`while (served < 3)`). It is a proof that the
// network reaches Koru on freestanding; it is not a server, and a load generator
// would outrun it before its first report.
//
// Measured 2026-08-09 by running the two commands above, in this directory.
// koru/examples/unikraft-net/BUILD.md has the QEMU line and the image sizes for
// the demo that does boot.

~import orisha

// The handler is implemented with a proc. `response { … }` used to be the
// spelling the library's docs showed; it was removed from the language on
// 2026-08-09 (koru 050b3549) because a single outcome does not get a name, so
// there is nothing to prefer this over any more — a handler returns the record.
~proc orisha:handler|zig {
    _ = &req;
    return .{
        .status = 200,
        .body = "Hello from Orisha, inside a unikernel.\n",
        .content_type = "text/plain",
    };
}

~orisha:serve(port: 8080)
| shutdown _ |> _
| failed _ |> _
